---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import blogLifeAbroad from '../assets/blog/life-abroad.jpg';
const title = "Your First Month Abroad: A Practical Checklist | CloudBridge";
const description = "What to do in your first week abroad, opening a bank account, coping with homesickness, and budgeting for your first month.";
---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<section class="py-10 bg-white border-b border-black/5">
  <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
    <p class="crumb !text-ink/40"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/blogs">Blogs</a> / Your First Month Abroad</p>
  </div>
</section>
<article class="py-10 md:py-14">
  <div class="max-w-container mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="course-badge">Life Abroad</p>
    <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-bridge-950 leading-tight">Your First Month Abroad: A Practical Checklist</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-ink/50">Updated July 2026 · 7 min read</p>
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <Image src={blogLifeAbroad} alt="A group of international students walking together on a university campus" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" widths={[480, 768, 1200]} sizes="(min-width: 768px) 700px, 100vw" />
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-lg text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Getting the visa and the flight booked is the hard part everyone prepares for — but the small logistics of the first few weeks (a bank account, a SIM card, figuring out where to buy groceries) are what actually make the first month abroad feel overwhelming. Here's the order to actually do things in, and what to expect.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">What should I do in my first week abroad?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Priority order matters more than most students expect. In roughly this order: register with your university (this usually unlocks your student ID and health cover), sort out accommodation logistics (keys, utilities, internet), open a local bank account, get a local SIM card, and register with a local doctor or health service if your country requires it. Everything else — furnishing your room, exploring the city, meeting people — can wait until these basics are handled.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">How do I open a bank account as an international student?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Most banks require your passport, proof of address (often a university or landlord letter), and your student visa or enrolment confirmation. Many universities partner with specific banks and can point you to a branch used to processing international student accounts quickly — worth asking your international student office in week one, since general high-street branches can be slower with unfamiliar paperwork.</p>
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl bg-bridge-50 p-5 border border-bridge-100">
      <p class="text-sm text-bridge-900"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Bring a small amount of cash in the local currency for your first few days — some processes (like a SIM card or a deposit) may need to happen before your new local bank account and card are fully active.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Is it normal to feel homesick, and how do I cope?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Yes — homesickness in the first few weeks is extremely common and not a sign you've made the wrong decision. It typically eases once you build a routine and a small social circle. Joining your university's international student society or a course-related club in the first two weeks is one of the most effective ways to speed that up, since it gives you a built-in group of people going through the exact same adjustment.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">How much money should I budget for my first month?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Budget for more than your usual monthly cost of living in that first month — you'll likely pay a rental deposit, buy basic kitchen and bedroom items, and cover a SIM card and transport card setup fee, on top of normal food and travel costs. A realistic buffer is 30-50% above your expected monthly living cost, specifically for that first month only.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Do I need a local SIM card and phone plan right away?</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink/70 leading-relaxed">Yes, ideally within the first day or two — you'll need a working local number for your bank account, university registration, and any local deliveries or appointments. Prepaid SIM plans are usually the easiest option initially, since they don't require a local credit history or bank account, which you likely won't have yet.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-display font-bold text-bridge-900">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 border border-black/10 rounded-2xl divide-y divide-black/10 bg-white">
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Should I bring bedding and kitchen items from home?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Generally no — luggage space is limited and these items are usually cheap and easy to buy locally in your first few days. Prioritise documents, a few days of clothing, and any essential medication instead.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">How do I meet people in a new country?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Orientation week events, course seminars, and university clubs are the fastest route — most international students make their first real friends through exactly these structured settings rather than by chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">What if I have a problem with my accommodation?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Contact your university's accommodation or international student office first — most universities have a dedicated process for this and can mediate with landlords far more effectively than you can alone as a new arrival.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <button class="faq-btn w-full text-left px-6 py-4 font-display font-semibold text-bridge-900 flex justify-between items-center">Can I work part-time from my first week?<span class="faq-icon transition-transform">+</span></button>
        <p class="faq-answer hidden px-6 pb-5 text-sm text-ink/70">Check your visa conditions first — most student visas do allow part-time work, but there's often a maximum weekly hour limit during term time that varies by country.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl bg-dusk-gradient text-white p-8 text-center">
      <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Want a full pre-departure checklist for your destination?</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-white/75 text-sm max-w-md mx-auto">Your CloudBridge counsellor can walk you through exactly what to expect in your first weeks — free, no obligation.</p>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="/#lead-form" class="btn-primary nav-link inline-flex">Get Free Consultation</a>
        <a href="/destinations" class="btn-secondary nav-link inline-flex">Explore Study Destinations</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8"><a href="/blogs" class="nav-link text-sm font-semibold text-bridge-800 hover:underline">&larr; Back to all articles</a></p>
  </div>
</article>
